        <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> Executive Summary & Core Mechanisms
      </div>
      <p>
        Statistical genetics formulates phenotype as a function of genotypes, environmental variance, and unobserved ancestry structures:
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Variance Decomposition:</strong> Phenotypic variance partitions into additive genetic, dominance, epistatic interaction, and environmental noise (<Katex tex="V_P = V_A + V_D + V_I + V_E" />).
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Linkage Disequilibrium:</strong> Non-random allele co-inheritance (<Katex tex="r^2, D'" />) creates correlated summary statistics, necessitating fine-mapping and LDSC.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Heritability & Mixed Models:</strong> Genomic Relatedness Matrices (<Katex tex="\mathbf{A}" />) and Linear Mixed Models (<Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \mathbf{u} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}" />) eliminate confounding while capturing polygenic architecture.
        <br/>
        &bull; <strong>Causality & Prediction:</strong> Instrumental variable techniques (Mendelian Randomization) test causal hypotheses, while PRS and neural sequence models (Borzoi, GPN-Star) bridge statistical association to molecular function.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="ch1">
      <h2>1. Foundations of Quantitative Genetics & The Infinitesimal Model</h2>
      <p>
        For decades after Gregor Mendel discovered discrete particulate inheritance (1866), a fierce intellectual battle raged between two schools of thought:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The Mendelians</strong> (William Bateson, Hugo de Vries): Argued that evolution occurs through large, discrete, qualitative mutations.</li>
        <li><strong>The Biometricians</strong> (Karl Pearson, Francis Galton): Argued that biological traits (e.g. human height, blood pressure, crop yield) follow smooth, continuous Gaussian distributions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The Fisher 1918 Synthesis</h3>
      <p>
        In a landmark paper, <strong>Ronald A. Fisher (1918)</strong> resolved the controversy by introducing the <strong>Infinitesimal Model</strong>. Fisher proved mathematically that if a trait is governed by an infinite number of Mendelian loci, each with an infinitesimally small additive effect, the sum of these independent alleles converges—by the Central Limit Theorem—to a continuous Gaussian distribution in the population.
      </p>

      <h3>Phenotypic Variance Decomposition</h3>
      <p>
        Consider a quantitative phenotype <Katex tex="y" /> measured across individuals in a population. Total phenotypic variance <Katex tex="V_P = \text{Var}(y)" /> decomposes linearly into genetic and environmental components:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Complete Phenotypic Variance Partitioning</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="V_P = V_G + V_E = V_A + V_D + V_I + V_E" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="V_A" /> = Additive genetic variance &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_D" /> = Dominance variance (intra-locus interaction) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_I" /> = Epistatic interaction variance (inter-locus interaction) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="V_E" /> = Environmental variance.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Narrow-Sense vs. Broad-Sense Heritability</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Broad-Sense Heritability (<Katex tex="H^2" />):</strong> The proportion of phenotypic variance attributable to all genetic factors combined:
          <div style="margin: 0.4rem 0; text-align: center;">
            <Katex tex="H^2 = \frac{V_G}{V_P} = \frac{V_A + V_D + V_I}{V_P}" display={true} />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Narrow-Sense Heritability (<Katex tex="h^2" />):</strong> The proportion of phenotypic variance explained solely by <strong>additive</strong> genetic effects:
          <div style="margin: 0.4rem 0; text-align: center;">
            <Katex tex="h^2 = \frac{V_A}{V_P}" display={true} />
          </div>
          <em>Why <Katex tex="h^2" /> is the central parameter of genetics:</em> Parents transmit single haploid gametes (alleles), not diploid genotypes or epistatic pairs. Thus, only additive variance <Katex tex="V_A" /> is reliably inherited from parent to offspring.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-math">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> The Breeder's Equation
        </div>
        <p>
          In evolutionary and agricultural genetics, the evolutionary response to selection <Katex tex="R" /> is directly proportional to narrow-sense heritability and the selection differential <Katex tex="S" />:
          <Katex tex="R = h^2 S" display={true} />
          where <Katex tex="S = \mu_{\text{selected}} - \mu_{\text{population}}" /> and <Katex tex="R = \mu_{\text{offspring}} - \mu_{\text{population}}" />.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium & Wright-Fisher Drift</h3>
      <p>
        At a biallelic locus with alleles <Katex tex="A" /> (frequency <Katex tex="p" />) and <Katex tex="a" /> (frequency <Katex tex="q = 1 - p" />), genotype frequencies under random mating, infinite population size, and no selection follow the binomial distribution:
        <Katex tex="P(AA) = p^2, \quad P(Aa) = 2pq, \quad P(aa) = q^2" display={true} />
        In finite populations of effective size <Katex tex="N_e" />, random sampling of gametes induces <strong>genetic drift</strong>, causing allele frequencies to fluctuate with variance <Katex tex="\text{Var}(\Delta p) = \frac{p(1-p)}{2N_e}" /> per generation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="ch2">
      <h2>2. Linkage Disequilibrium (LD) & Chromosomal Architecture</h2>
      <p>
        During meiosis, homologous chromosomes cross over and recombine. When two loci are physically adjacent on a chromosome, recombination between them is rare, causing their alleles to be co-inherited as correlated haplotypes. This non-random association of alleles at different loci is termed <strong>Linkage Disequilibrium (LD)</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3>Mathematical Quantification of LD</h3>
      <p>
        Consider two biallelic loci: Locus 1 with alleles <Katex tex="A/a" /> (frequencies <Katex tex="p_A, p_a" />) and Locus 2 with alleles <Katex tex="B/b" /> (frequencies <Katex tex="p_B, p_b" />).
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Linkage Disequilibrium Parameters: D, D', and r²</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="D = P(AB) - p_A p_B" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="D' = \frac{D}{D_{\max}}, \quad D_{\max} = \begin{cases} \min(p_A p_b, p_a p_B) & \text{if } D > 0 \\ \min(p_A p_B, p_a p_b) & \text{if } D < 0 \end{cases}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
          <Katex tex="r^2 = \frac{D^2}{p_A (1 - p_A) p_B (1 - p_B)}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="D = 0" /> represents complete equilibrium &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="|D'| = 1" /> indicates complete absence of observed historical recombination (only 3 of 4 possible haplotypes exist) &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="r^2" /> = Squared Pearson correlation coefficient (determines statistical power in association testing).
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Decay of LD Over Time & Recombination Hotspots</h3>
      <p>
        Over <Katex tex="t" /> generations with recombination fraction <Katex tex="\theta" />, disequilibrium decays exponentially:
        <Katex tex="D_t = D_0 (1 - \theta)^t \approx D_0 e^{-\theta t}" display={true} />
        In the human genome, meiotic recombination does not occur uniformly. Over 80% of cross-overs are concentrated into narrow 1–2 kb <strong>recombination hotspots</strong> directed by the sequence-specific zinc-finger binding protein <strong>PRDM9</strong>. Between hotspots lie discrete <strong>haplotype blocks</strong> characterized by high internal <Katex tex="r^2" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="ch3">
      <h2>3. Heritability Estimation: From Twin Studies to Genomic REML (GREML)</h2>

      <h3>The Twin Study Paradigm</h3>
      <p>
        Historically, human heritability was estimated by comparing phenotypic correlations between Monozygotic (MZ, sharing 100% of genes) and Dizygotic (DZ, sharing on average 50% of segregating alleles) twin pairs. Under the Equal Environments Assumption, <strong>Falconer's formula</strong> computes:
        <Katex tex="h^2 = 2(r_{\text{MZ}} - r_{\text{DZ}})" display={true} />
        Twin studies consistently revealed high heritability across human traits: height (<Katex tex="h^2 \approx 0.80" />), schizophrenia (<Katex tex="h^2 \approx 0.80" />), and BMI (<Katex tex="h^2 \approx 0.70" />).
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> The Missing Heritability Dilemma
        </div>
        <p>
          When initial GWAS were conducted in the late 2000s, genome-wide significant SNPs explained only a tiny fraction of twin-estimated heritability (e.g. ~5% for human height despite <Katex tex="h^2 \approx 80\%" />). This gap was termed the <strong>"Missing Heritability"</strong> problem.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>The GCTA / GREML Breakthrough</h3>
      <p>
        In 2010, <strong>Jian Yang, Peter Visscher, and colleagues</strong> introduced <strong>Genomic-REML (GREML)</strong> implemented in the software <strong>GCTA</strong> (Genome-wide Complex Trait Analysis). They demonstrated that the missing heritability was not missing from common variants, but was dispersed across hundreds of thousands of common SNPs whose individual effect sizes were too small to reach genome-wide significance (<Katex tex="p < 5 \times 10^{-8}" />).
      </p>

      <h3>The Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM)</h3>
      <p>
        For <Katex tex="N" /> unrelated individuals genotyped across <Katex tex="M" /> SNPs, let <Katex tex="X_{ij} \in \{0, 1, 2\}" /> be the genotype of individual <Katex tex="i" /> at SNP <Katex tex="j" /> with allele frequency <Katex tex="p_j" />. The standardized GRM <Katex tex="\mathbf{A} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}" /> is:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Genomic Relatedness Matrix (GRM)</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="A_{ik} = \frac{1}{M} \sum_{j=1}^{M} \frac{(X_{ij} - 2p_j)(X_{kj} - 2p_j)}{2p_j(1 - p_j)}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Genomic Restricted Maximum Likelihood (GREML)</h3>
      <p>
        Treating individual genetic values as random effects:
        <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \mathbf{g} + \mathbf{e}, \quad \mathbf{g} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{A}\sigma_g^2), \quad \mathbf{e} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{I}\sigma_e^2)" display={true} />
        The variance-covariance matrix of the phenotype is:
        <Katex tex="\mathbf{V} = \mathbf{A}\sigma_g^2 + \mathbf{I}\sigma_e^2" display={true} />
        Maximizing the Restricted Log-Likelihood (REML) yields unbiased estimates of <Katex tex="\sigma_g^2" /> and <Katex tex="\sigma_e^2" />, defining <strong>SNP-heritability</strong>:
        <Katex tex="h^2_{\text{SNP}} = \frac{\sigma_g^2}{\sigma_g^2 + \sigma_e^2}" display={true} />
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="ch4">
      <h2>4. Summary-Statistics Heritability: LD Score Regression (LDSC & S-LDSC)</h2>
      <p>
        While GREML requires individual-level genotype matrices (demanding massive memory for <Katex tex="N > 100,000" />), <strong>LD Score Regression (LDSC)</strong> (Bulik-Sullivan et al., 2015) estimates heritability and confounding using only summary statistics (<Katex tex="Z" />-scores or <Katex tex="\chi^2" /> statistics).
      </p>

      <h3>Defining the LD Score</h3>
      <p>
        The LD score <Katex tex="\ell_j" /> of SNP <Katex tex="j" /> measures the total amount of genetic variation tagged by that SNP:
        <Katex tex="\ell_j = \sum_{k=1}^{M} r_{jk}^2" display={true} />
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">The Fundamental LDSC Theorem</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi^2_j] = 1 + \frac{N h^2}{M} \ell_j + N a" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\chi^2_j = z_j^2" /> = GWAS chi-squared test statistic &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="N" /> = Sample size &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="M" /> = Number of SNPs &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="a" /> = Population stratification / cryptic relatedness confounding parameter.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-math">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> Disentangling Polygenicity from Confounding
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>True Polygenic Signal:</strong> Correlates linearly with the LD score <Katex tex="\ell_j" /> (slope <Katex tex="\propto h^2" />) because a SNP tagging more genome captures more causal variance by chance.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Population Stratification Confounding:</strong> Inflates test statistics uniformly across the entire genome, independent of local LD (shifting the intercept <Katex tex="1 + Na > 1" />).
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Cross-Trait Genetic Correlation (<Katex tex="r_g" />)</h3>
      <p>
        Bivariate LDSC computes the genetic correlation between two traits using the product of their <Katex tex="Z" />-scores:
        <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[z_{1j} z_{2j}] = \frac{\sqrt{N_1 N_2} \rho_g}{M} \ell_j + \frac{\rho N_s}{\sqrt{N_1 N_2}}" display={true} />
        where <Katex tex="N_s" /> is the number of shared samples between the two GWAS cohorts and <Katex tex="\rho" /> is phenotypic correlation. The genetic correlation is:
        <Katex tex="r_g = \frac{\rho_g}{\sqrt{h_1^2 h_2^2}}" display={true} />
      </p>

      <h3>Stratified LD Score Regression (S-LDSC)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Stratified LDSC (S-LDSC)</strong> (Finucane et al., 2015) partitions heritability across <Katex tex="C" /> functional genomic annotations (e.g. coding exons, enhancers, conserved elements, cell-type specific chromatin):
        <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi^2_j] = 1 + N \sum_{c=1}^C \tau_c \ell_{j,c}" display={true} />
        where <Katex tex="\ell_{j,c} = \sum_{k \in \text{Category } c} r_{jk}^2" /> is the stratified LD score, and <Katex tex="\tau_c" /> is the per-SNP contribution to heritability of annotation <Katex tex="c" />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="ch5">
      <h2>5. Association Testing & Linear Mixed Models (LMM)</h2>

      <h3>Marginal Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) Regression</h3>
      <p>
        In a standard GWAS, each SNP <Katex tex="j" /> is tested individually against phenotype <Katex tex="y" />:
        <Katex tex="y_i = \alpha + x_{ij} \beta_j + \sum_{k=1}^K \gamma_k z_{ik} + \epsilon_i" display={true} />
        where <Katex tex="z_{ik}" /> are covariates (e.g. age, sex, top 10–20 genetic principal components). The Wald test statistic is:
        <Katex tex="t_j = \frac{\hat{\beta}_j}{\text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_j)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 1) \quad \implies \quad \chi^2_j = t_j^2 \sim \chi^2_1" display={true} />
      </p>

      <h3>Confounding Structures in Association Testing</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Population Stratification:</strong> Ancestral allele frequency differences correlated with environmental or lifestyle differences (the classic "chopsticks" gene artifact).</li>
        <li><strong>Cryptic Relatedness & Family Structure:</strong> Distant relatedness among cohort participants violating the independence assumption of OLS residuals.</li>
        <li><strong>Assortative Mating & Dynastic Effects:</strong> Non-random mate choice and parental genotype nurturing effects.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Linear Mixed Models (LMM)</h3>
      <p>
        Linear Mixed Models simultaneously test the candidate SNP as a fixed effect while modeling the entire genome-wide polygenic background as a random effect:
        <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \mathbf{Z}\mathbf{u} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}, \quad \mathbf{u} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{K}\sigma_g^2), \quad \boldsymbol{\epsilon} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \mathbf{I}\sigma_e^2)" display={true} />
        where <Katex tex="\mathbf{K}" /> is the kinship / genomic relationship matrix.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-table-wrap">
        <table class="deep-dive-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Method</th>
              <th>Algorithmic Innovation</th>
              <th>Computational Complexity</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Exact LMM / GEMMA</strong></td>
              <td>Spectral decomposition (eigenvalue decomposition) of kinship matrix <Katex tex="\mathbf{K}" />.</td>
              <td><Katex tex="\mathcal{O}(N^3)" /> setup, <Katex tex="\mathcal{O}(N^2 M)" /> testing.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>BOLT-LMM (Loh et al. 2015)</strong></td>
              <td>Conjugate gradient iterations + Monte Carlo REML; spike-and-slab mixture prior for random effects.</td>
              <td><Katex tex="\mathcal{O}(M N^{1.5})" /> (Scales to <Katex tex="N \approx 500,000" />).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>fastGWA (Jiang et al. 2019)</strong></td>
              <td>Sparse Genomic Relatedness Matrix (pruning distant pairs with <Katex tex="A_{ik} < 0.05" />).</td>
              <td>Linear in <Katex tex="N" /> via Cholesky decomposition of sparse matrix.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Regenie (Mbatchou et al. 2021)</strong></td>
              <td>Two-step stacked block-ridge regression for whole-genome predictions; logistic Firth regression for rare case-control events.</td>
              <td>Ultra-fast, ultra-low memory across millions of traits.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="ch6">
      <h2>6. Bayesian Fine-Mapping & Causal Variant Prioritization</h2>
      <p>
        Because GWAS association signals are smeared across dozens of LD-correlated variants, observing <Katex tex="p < 5 \times 10^{-8}" /> at a SNP does not mean that SNP is biologically causal.
      </p>

      <h3>The Fundamental LD Confounding Equation</h3>
      <p>
        Let <Katex tex="\boldsymbol{\beta}_{\text{true}}" /> be the vector of true causal effect sizes at a locus, and let <Katex tex="\mathbf{R}" /> be the <Katex tex="M \times M" /> pairwise LD correlation matrix (<Katex tex="R_{jk} = r_{jk}" />). The observed marginal GWAS effect size vector <Katex tex="\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}" /> satisfies:
        <Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\hat{\boldsymbol{\beta}}] = \mathbf{R} \boldsymbol{\beta}_{\text{true}}" display={true} />
      </p>

      <h3>Sum of Single Effects (SuSiE)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>SuSiE (Wang & Stephens, 2020)</strong> formulates fine-mapping as a sum of <Katex tex="L" /> distinct single-effect linear regression vectors:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">SuSiE Formulation</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \sum_{l=1}^L \mathbf{X} \boldsymbol{\gamma}_l b_l + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}, \quad \boldsymbol{\gamma}_l \sim \text{Multinomial}(1, \boldsymbol{\pi}), \quad b_l \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_{0,l}^2)" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\boldsymbol{\gamma}_l \in \{0, 1\}^M" /> = Indicator vector selecting exactly one causal SNP for effect <Katex tex="l" /> &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\boldsymbol{\pi}" /> = Prior inclusion probability vector &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="b_l" /> = Effect size.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Posterior Inclusion Probabilities (PIP) & 95% Credible Sets</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Posterior Inclusion Probability (PIP):</strong> The probability that SNP <Katex tex="j" /> is causal across any of the <Katex tex="L" /> effects:
          <Katex tex="\text{PIP}_j = P(\text{SNP } j \text{ is causal} \mid \text{Data}) = 1 - \prod_{l=1}^L (1 - \gamma_{l, j})" display={true} />
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>95% Credible Set (CS):</strong> The minimal set of variants that contains the true causal variant with at least 95% cumulative posterior probability:
          <Katex tex="\sum_{j \in \text{CS}_l} \alpha_{l, j} \ge 0.95" display={true} />
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Functional Priors (PolyFun & Sniff)</h3>
      <p>
        Instead of assuming an uninformative uniform prior (<Katex tex="\pi_j = 1/M" />), modern fine-mapping frameworks (such as <strong>PolyFun</strong> and <strong>Sniff</strong>) estimate variant-specific prior probabilities by integrating functional genomics annotations (e.g. S-LDSC weights, Borzoi sequence-to-function predictions):
        <Katex tex="\pi_j = \frac{1}{1 + \exp\left(-\left(\alpha + \sum_c \tau_c a_{jc}\right)\right)}" display={true} />
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="ch7">
      <h2>7. Rare Variant Association Testing (RVAT)</h2>
      <p>
        As whole-exome (WES) and whole-genome sequencing (WGS) scale to hundreds of thousands of individuals (e.g. UK Biobank 450k WES), geneticists increasingly study rare variants (<Katex tex="\text{MAF} < 0.1\%" />). Single-marker tests suffer from severe statistical power loss due to low minor allele counts.
      </p>

      <h3>Gene-Level Aggregation Tests</h3>
      <p>
        RVAT aggregates multiple rare variants across a gene or functional region:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-table-wrap">
        <table class="deep-dive-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Test Type</th>
              <th>Test Statistic Formulation</th>
              <th>Optimal Biological Regime</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Burden Test (CAST / CMC)</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="T_{\text{burden}} = \sum_{j=1}^m w_j \hat{\beta}_j" /> (Sums weighted genotypes into a single collapsing variable).</td>
              <td>High proportion of causal variants, all acting in the <strong>same direction</strong> (e.g. loss-of-function nonsense mutations).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>SKAT (Wu et al. 2011)</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\mathcal{Q}_{\text{SKAT}} = \sum_{j=1}^m w_j^2 S_j^2 = (\mathbf{y} - \hat{\mathbf{y}}_0)^T \mathbf{K} (\mathbf{y} - \hat{\mathbf{y}}_0)" /> (Variance-component score test).</td>
              <td>Mixture of protective, deleterious, and neutral variants acting in <strong>bidirectional</strong> effect regimes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>SKAT-O (Optimal)</strong></td>
              <td><Katex tex="\mathcal{Q}_{\rho} = \rho \mathcal{Q}_{\text{burden}} + (1 - \rho) \mathcal{Q}_{\text{SKAT}}" /> (Optimizes mixture parameter <Katex tex="\rho \in [0, 1]" />).</td>
              <td>Robust across unknown mixtures of directional and bidirectional effects.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>DeepRVAT (Song et al. 2024)</strong></td>
              <td>Deep set neural aggregator trained on AlphaMissense, ESM-1v, and GPN-Star embeddings.</td>
              <td>Non-linear functional variant prioritization in whole-exome sequencing cohorts.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 8 -->
    <section id="ch8">
      <h2>8. Polygenic Risk Scores (PRS) & Disease Prediction</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>Polygenic Risk Score (PRS)</strong> quantifies an individual's genetic liability to a phenotype by summing risk alleles weighted by their GWAS effect sizes:
        <Katex tex="\text{PRS}_i = \sum_{j=1}^M \hat{\beta}_j G_{ij}" display={true} />
      </p>

      <h3>From Clumping & Thresholding to Bayesian Shrinkage</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Clumping & Thresholding (<Katex tex="C+T" />):</strong> Prunes LD-correlated SNPs (<Katex tex="r^2 < r^2_{\text{thresh}}" />) and filters by <Katex tex="p" />-value threshold (<Katex tex="p < p_T" />). Simple, but discards valuable polygenic signals in LD.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>LDpred / LDpred2 (Vilhjálmsson et al., Privé et al.):</strong> Employs a Bayesian Gaussian spike-and-slab mixture prior over true effect sizes <Katex tex="\beta_j" />:
          <Katex tex="\beta_j \sim (1 - p) \delta_0 + p \mathcal{N}\left(0, \frac{h^2}{M p}\right)" display={true} />
          Gibbs sampling computes the posterior mean effect conditioned on reference LD.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>PRS-CS (Ge et al. 2019):</strong> Applies a continuous global-local shrinkage prior (Strawderman-Berger / Dirichlet-Laplace) to achieve robust shrinkage without MCMC mixing failures.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-warning">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="alert-triangle" size={15} /> The Trans-Ethnic Portability Crisis
        </div>
        <p>
          PRSs trained on European-ancestry discovery cohorts (e.g. UK Biobank) suffer an <strong>approximate 50–70% drop in predictive accuracy (<Katex tex="R^2" />)</strong> when applied to African, East Asian, or Hispanic ancestries due to differences in LD architecture, allele frequencies, and causal effect heterogeneity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 9 -->
    <section id="ch9">
      <h2>9. Causal Inference in Genetics: Mendelian Randomization (MR)</h2>
      <p>
        In observational epidemiology, determining whether a biomarker (e.g. LDL cholesterol) directly causes a disease (e.g. coronary artery disease) is often confounded by reverse causation and unmeasured lifestyle factors. <strong>Mendelian Randomization (MR)</strong> uses genetic variants as <strong>Instrumental Variables (IVs)</strong> to establish causal inference.
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-math">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="sparkles" size={15} /> Nature's Randomized Controlled Trial
        </div>
        <p>
          Because alleles are randomly assorted and fixed at conception (Mendel's Law of Independent Assortment), genotypes act as lifelong randomized exposures that are immune to reverse causation and environmental confounding.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>The Three Core Instrumental Variable Assumptions</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Relevance:</strong> The genetic variant <Katex tex="Z" /> is strongly associated with the intermediate exposure <Katex tex="X" /> (first-stage <Katex tex="F\text{-statistic} > 10" />).</li>
        <li><strong>Independence:</strong> <Katex tex="Z" /> is independent of all confounders <Katex tex="U" /> between exposure <Katex tex="X" /> and outcome <Katex tex="Y" />.</li>
        <li><strong>Exclusion Restriction:</strong> <Katex tex="Z" /> affects the outcome <Katex tex="Y" /> <em>only</em> through the exposure <Katex tex="X" /> (no direct pleiotropic pathways).</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">Inverse-Variance Weighted (IVW) Estimator</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{\text{MR}} = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^M w_j \hat{\beta}_{Xj} \hat{\beta}_{Yj}}{\sum_{j=1}^M w_j \hat{\beta}_{Xj}^2}, \quad w_j = \frac{1}{\text{Var}(\hat{\beta}_{Yj})}" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{Xj}" /> = Variant effect on exposure &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{Yj}" /> = Variant effect on outcome &emsp;|&emsp;
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{\text{MR}}" /> = Estimated causal effect of exposure on outcome.
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>Violations & Robust MR Methods</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Horizontal Pleiotropy:</strong> When a SNP affects the outcome through a biological pathway independent of the exposure, violating the Exclusion Restriction assumption.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>MR-Egger Regression:</strong> Fits a regression with an unconstrained intercept (<Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{Yj} = \beta_0 + \beta_{\text{MR}} \hat{\beta}_{Xj}" />). A non-zero intercept (<Katex tex="\beta_0 \neq 0" />) directly tests and corrects for directional horizontal pleiotropy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Weighted Median Estimator:</strong> Provides a consistent causal estimate even when up to 50% of the genetic instruments are invalid.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 10 -->
    <section id="ch10">
      <h2>10. The Modern Synthesis: Statistical Genetics Meets Deep Learning</h2>
      <p>
        Statistical genetics and deep learning sequence-to-function modeling are converging into a unified computational discipline:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-biology">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="activity" size={15} /> The Unified Modern Synthesis
        </div>
        <p>
          &bull; <strong>Neural S-LDSC & Sniff:</strong> Sequence models (Borzoi, Enformer) predict 7,000+ functional genomics tracks, generating continuous annotations for S-LDSC and SuSiE fine-mapping that outperform static binary peaks.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Phylogeny-Aware Language Models (GPN-Star):</strong> Transformer language models conditioned on whole-genome alignments and species trees capture purifying selection across vertebrate, mammalian, and primate timescales.
          <br/>
          &bull; <strong>Neural Aggregation in RVAT (DeepRVAT):</strong> Deep set networks integrate structural embeddings (AlphaFold/AlphaMissense) and sequence models to uncover novel disease genes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h3>Master Statistical Genetics Cheat-Sheet</h3>
      <div class="deep-dive-table-wrap">
        <table class="deep-dive-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Task / Problem</th>
              <th>Standard Methodology</th>
              <th>Key Formula / Metric</th>
              <th>Primary Software Tools</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Heritability Estimation</strong></td>
              <td>GREML & LDSC</td>
              <td><Katex tex="\mathbb{E}[\chi^2_j] = 1 + \frac{N h^2}{M}\ell_j + Na" /></td>
              <td>GCTA, LDSC, S-LDSC</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Genome-Wide Association</strong></td>
              <td>Linear Mixed Models (LMM)</td>
              <td><Katex tex="\mathbf{y} = \mathbf{X}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \mathbf{u} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}" /></td>
              <td>BOLT-LMM, fastGWA, Regenie, PLINK2</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Bayesian Fine-Mapping</strong></td>
              <td>Sum of Single Effects (SuSiE)</td>
              <td><Katex tex="\text{PIP}_j = 1 - \prod (1 - \gamma_{lj})" /></td>
              <td>SuSiE, PolyFun, FINEMAP, Sniff</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Rare Variant Testing</strong></td>
              <td>SKAT-O & Neural RVAT</td>
              <td><Katex tex="\mathcal{Q}_{\rho} = \rho \mathcal{Q}_{\text{B}} + (1 - \rho)\mathcal{Q}_{\text{SKAT}}" /></td>
              <td>SKAT, STAAR, DeepRVAT</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Polygenic Prediction</strong></td>
              <td>Bayesian Shrinkage PRS</td>
              <td><Katex tex="\text{PRS}_i = \sum \hat{\beta}_j G_{ij}" /></td>
              <td>LDpred2, PRS-CS, Lassosum</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Causal Inference</strong></td>
              <td>Mendelian Randomization</td>
              <td><Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{\text{MR}} = \frac{\sum w_j \hat{\beta}_{Xj}\hat{\beta}_{Yj}}{\sum w_j \hat{\beta}_{Xj}^2}" /></td>
              <td>TwoSampleMR, MendelianRandomization</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
